      "problem": "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x+1$ and $v(x)=2{x}^{2}-11x+5,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4x-11.\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x){v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot (2{x}^{2}-11x+5)-(x+1)\\cdot (4x-11)}{{(2{x}^{2}-11x+5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-11x+5-(4{x}^{2}-11x+4x-11)}{{(2{x}^{2}-11x+5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-11x+5-4{x}^{2}+11x-4x+11}{{(2{x}^{2}-11x+5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{x}^{2}-4x+16}{{(2{x}^{2}-11x+5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2({x}^{2}+2x-8)}{{(2{x}^{2}-11x+5)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
